1
00:00:03,225 --> 00:00:12,395
Prescribe and send electronically — Clinical ▸ Diagnoses ▸ e-Prescribe — Write a prescription, check the chart, send it to the pharmacy.

2
00:00:17,877 --> 00:00:38,493
Prescribing starts from the diagnosis — now under Clinical — not from a blank drug field: the medication should always have a documented reason.

3
00:00:38,493 --> 00:00:51,350
Diagnosis coded. Now the treatment that follows from it.

4
00:00:51,350 --> 00:01:03,698
Medications are searched, not free-typed — that is what keeps the name, strength and form unambiguous.

5
00:01:03,698 --> 00:01:11,624
Dose and frequency are what the pharmacy dispenses against. Be exact.

6
00:01:11,624 --> 00:01:17,008
Quantity and refills together decide how long the patient goes before they need you again.

7
00:01:17,008 --> 00:01:24,844
Prescribing here does not absolve you of the check. Sarah has a documented penicillin allergy and is already on Lisinopril.

8
00:01:24,844 --> 00:01:35,967
Both are on her chart, one tab away. Read the allergy list and the current medications before you send — that is the habit this screen is for.

9
00:01:35,967 --> 00:01:46,872
Patient instructions print on the label — write them for the patient, not for the chart.

10
00:01:46,872 --> 00:01:53,684
Sent electronically. It is on the chart, in the patient’s portal, and at the pharmacy before they have left the building.

11
00:01:53,771 --> 00:02:08,251
What you just did

12
00:02:08,874 --> 00:02:17,874
Health | Efficiency | Growth — More in the Getting Started series — the next video is linked on screen.
